+## Pooled constants (v2, `js-pool-consts`)
+
+`-Cllvm-args=js-pool-consts=on|off` (default off; on in `js-profile=release-size` and
+`release-balanced`, off in `release-speed`, which keeps item names) makes the JIR lane emit large
+immutable constant data that only shared references reach **once**, as a module level `const`,
+and name it at every use. It rewrites what is printed, so it takes effect under `js-opt=on` only
+(`off` and `report` print what they always did). It is **link-only**: the decision is made over the whole program at link
+time (`jsc-link/src/link/pool.rs`), objects are identical either way, and it is not in the
+sysroot key. Adding the field moved the positional producer/linker manifest to version 6, whose
+version `jsc-link` reads before it decodes anything else, so a mismatched dylib and linker are
+told apart from a corrupt manifest. The host-exposure rule reads rustc's `Freeze` answer, which
+the producer carries as `TypeMeta::freeze` from JIR schema 16. The baseline lane never pools.
+
+**Why it exists.** A `const T: &[(&str, &[&str])] = &[..]` is a constant of reference type: every
+use is a MIR constant operand pointing into one promoted, immutable allocation, and
+`C.iter()` on a by-value `const C: [_; N]` borrows a promoted `&C` the same way. The lowering
+spelled the decoded allocation as a fresh literal at every operand, so FingerprintJS's 45-entry
+`BLOCKER_FILTERS` table was in the program four times and its `FONT_PREFERENCES` twice.
+
+**The rule.** A constant node is pooled when all of the following hold:
+
+1. it is the pointee of a pointer into anonymous memory (`ConstTree::Reference`/`Slice`), which the
+   producer encodes only for allocations rustc interned immutable
+   (`backend/src/jir/encode.rs::check_const_memory` declines `static mut` and interior-mutable
+   memory, nested allocations included), so nothing may write it;
+2. its position is a **shared** reference: not `&mut` and not a raw pointer, which could be cast to
+   a writable one. A by-value constant (`let mut a = C;`) is a fresh value per use and is never
+   pooled; only the shared data inside it can be. The `{ buf, off, len }` record of a slice is the
+   pointer and stays per use; its backing array is what is pooled. A reference to an aggregate is
+   the aggregate's own object, so that object is pooled;
+3. nothing in it names a `static`, so a binding reads only literals and hoisted functions and has
+   no initialization order of its own. Data behind a shared reference is never dropped;
+4. host code cannot reach it by any route. A pooled object is one object for every use, so host
+   code that could reach it could change what every other use reads, where it used to change a
+   copy of its own. The link step computes the footprint of what host code can reach
+   (`jsc-link/src/link/pool.rs::Exposure`), as the set of object types reached and the pointee
+   types of the slot pointers reached (a slot is a container plus a key, so its holder holds the
+   container), and declines a candidate whose objects meet those objects, or whose objects or
+   places meet those slots. What counts:
+   * every argument of every call the lowering intercepts, and what such a call returns (Rust may
+     write into it or call through it): `#[js_extern]` declarations, `js!{}` blocks, the
+     `view-abi`, template and keyed-list markers, the `rcgjs.map.*` collections (which store keys
+     and values in a host `Map` the program can return or pass on), every `rcgjs.host.*` marker
+     (a function of the program's own may carry any marker's `link_section`, so `mires-js`'s
+     sealed signatures are not relied on), and a marker that reached the encoder without a
+     payload;
+   * every C-ABI foreign call whose symbol is not a member of the shipped runtime shim: it lowers
+     to `__rt.<symbol>(..)`, which host code defined;
+   * three exceptions, from what the lowering emits: a closure marker (`callback0`–`4`,
+     `promise`, `localwaker`, `microtask`, `task`, `try`, `settled`) hands the host an arrow that
+     closes over the environment, so what counts is the closure body's result and its arguments;
+     a future driver (`spawn`, `spawnowner`, `futurepromise`, `futurenativepromise`) keeps the
+     future in the runtime's own closure state, so what counts is the output its `poll_*` helpers
+     return; `u8arrayfrombytes`/`u8arrayintobytes` copy numbers, so a byte slice in the copied
+     position is not reached (any other type there is);
+   * an exported or rooted item's result, and every argument its body reads (one it never reads
+     can be neither written nor called through), as host-supplied data: Rust may write into a
+     by-value aggregate, through a `&mut`, through a raw pointer, and through a shared reference
+     to a type that is not `Freeze` (`TypeMeta::freeze`, rustc's answer, carried since JIR schema
+     16); it may call host functions it finds there, handing them their arguments. An exported
+     `static` counts both ways.
+
+   A footprint the walk cannot bound — a `dyn`, an opaque or foreign type, a raw pointer to an
+   erased pointee handed over, a closure or helper body the program lacks, a function without a
+   JIR body — declines every candidate. A panic handler that reads its `&PanicInfo` is one: it is
+   exported, and `PanicInfo` reaches `fmt` function pointers Rust may call with an erased pointer
+   and a `&mut Formatter` (`dyn Write`). So is a program that spawns futures on `mires-js`'s
+   executor: the `Waker` its `localwaker` marker returns is host-supplied data whose vtable functions Rust
+   calls with the waker's erased data pointer, so five of the ten browser workloads pool nothing
+   (none of them had an eligible constant either). The shipped runtime shim's own members are
+   trusted; replacing them is outside this model;
+5. it prints to at least 256 bytes compact and the scan finds at least two uses of it. DEFLATE's
+   longest match is 258 bytes, so a shorter repeat within its window is one back-reference to
+   gzip (and cheaper to Brotli) that a declaration plus a name per use does not beat. On the
+   compact FingerprintJS package, pooling its 218-byte font table too saved 210 bytes minified
+   but cost 11 bytes gzipped and 73 Brotli; pooling single uses cost 194 bytes gzipped and 59
+   Brotli for 12 minified; at both thresholds measured on the ten browser workloads (64 and 256
+   bytes) every workload came out byte-identical (none repeats an eligible constant).
+
+Pointer identity does not constrain it: Rust guarantees neither distinct addresses for two
+promoted constants nor equal addresses for two uses of one `const`, so one object per value is a
+legal answer. Data of different types never shares a binding even when it prints alike: the name
+is `pool$h<hash>` of the type and the compact text together, so it is the same in every crate and
+read order, and a hash collision declines both values.
+
+**Where the binding goes.** It is an `ItemKind::Const` item like a hoisted constant: placed among
+the bindings before any binding that reads it (a `static` initializer evaluated at module load
+included), kept alive by its users' `refs` and dropped with the last of them, owned like any item
+by the chunk that reaches it or by the shared chunk when two do, shortened by the item rename
+under `js-minify=on`, and without source mappings of its own (its users' mappings are
+unchanged). A chunk places only the items it owns (`link::order`): a binding it owns that reads
+one another chunk owns comes after the `import` of it and never declares it again. Before that
+rule a chunk-owned `static` reading a shared binding declared the shared one a second time, a
+`SyntaxError`, with or without pooling. The
+function references in its data are in its own `refs`, so the reachability walk still sees them;
+the JIR analyses (call graph, value flow, escape) ran on the constant trees before lowering and
+are unaffected. Only the final phase-C lowering pools; the physical ABI's cost trials and the
+representation probes print every constant inline as before.
+
+**Residuals.** Not followed: a value reinterpreted through an `unsafe` cast between non-erased
+pointee types (a reference transmuted into a `JsValue`, a `*const T` read as a `*const U`), and a
+write through a raw pointer to an erased pointee that host code supplied. Both need `unsafe`.
+
+**Known wrong answer, not the pool's.** `let mut p = TABLE[4]; p.x = 99;` with `p` never read
+again emits the copy as an alias of the element (`e=f[g+b];e.x=99;`), so the write lands in the
+table. That is a miscompile of the copy itself — the same program over a `static`, a local array
+or a slice parameter is wrong without pooling — and it is being fixed separately; with pooling
+the table is the shared binding, so the write is seen by every reader. Fixture 121 carries the
+shape with the native answer and fails until that fix is in.
+
+The lane sidecar's `pool` block (schema 18) lists each binding with its bytes and scanned and
+emitted uses, the declines by reason, whether the host footprint was bounded (and why not), and
+the repeated by-value constants left per use (case b, never pooled). `scripts/pooled-constants-test.sh`
+is the executable contract: fixture `121_pooled_constants` (tables read by several functions and
+closures, a by-value copy written in place, function pointers called through a table, identical
+interior-mutable statics, identical data of two types, statics initialized from pooled data, a
+raw-pointer table and a static-naming table that are never pooled, the element copy above),
+`js-pool-consts=off` as the control, the source map, a two-chunk split whose chunk-owned statics
+read a pooled and a shared binding, and the host-exposure controls `examples/pooled_host_exposure.rs`
+(a `#[js_extern]` argument, a `HashMap` returned to the host, the program's own `rcgjs.host.erase`
+marker, a host-supplied `Cell`) and `examples/pooled_host_symbol.rs` (a host-defined C-ABI
+symbol). Four mutants, which only a `jsc-link` built with debug assertions honours
+(`RCGJS_POOL_FAULT=by-value|mut-ref|static|host`), must each be caught.
